Look up a stateless reset without processing the datagram.

Applies to picoquic 7e77d01d9ec4fd03f71529637549863917aeb793, after 0001 to
0003.

RFC 9000 Section 10.3.1 has an endpoint compare a datagram's last 16 bytes
with the stateless reset tokens registered for the remote address it came
from. An application that runs several QUIC contexts on one port receives a
datagram on one context while the token may belong to a connection on
another. picoquic_detect_stateless_reset(quic, bytes, length, addr_from,
current_time, &detection) performs only that comparison, against the
(address, token) pairs the context has registered:

- It processes no packet and sends nothing. A match applies the reset to the
  connection the token names: the disconnected state is committed before any
  callback, then the stateless_reset and close callbacks run, each read from
  the registration at that step. The result is
  picoquic_reset_detection_none, _reset or _already_disconnected.
- Datagrams shorter than PICOQUIC_RESET_PACKET_MIN_DETECT_SIZE (21) cannot
  match. The comparison is constant time on every platform.
- The registered pair is refreshed whenever the default path's first tuple
  changes, including the path-0 replacement when a multipath path is
  abandoned, so the lookup finds the token in use.

Ordinary ingress keeps its convention: picoquic_incoming_stateless_reset
applies the same reset and returns PICOQUIC_ERROR_AEAD_CHECK.
Tests (picoquic_ct): stateless_reset_detect, stateless_reset_detect_callback,
stateless_reset_detect_vn, stateless_reset_detect_secondary,
stateless_reset_detect_rotated and stateless_reset_detect_abandon. In LibMoQ:
the threaded_lane_group_reset_* rows.

